<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chavundaraya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">10th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chavundaraya was a minister and military commander of the Western Ganga kings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marasimha-ii">Marasimha II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rachamalla-iv">Rachamalla IV</a> in the late 10th century. A devout Jain, he commissioned around 983 the giant statue of Bahubali, known as Gommateshwara, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shravanabelagola">Shravanabelagola</a>. He also wrote the Chavundaraya Purana, one of the earliest surviving prose works in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chavundaraya.jpg" alt="Chavundaraya" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: HoysalaPhotos · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frontal_south_eastern_view_of_the_Chavundaraya_Basadi_at_Shravanabelagola.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>In his own writing Chavundaraya claims descent from the Brahmakshatriya line, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> who had taken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshatriya">Kshatriya</a> status. The 10th-century Algodu and Arani inscriptions name him as the grandson of Govindamayya and the son of Mabalayya, a subordinate of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marasimha-ii">Marasimha II</a>; the family's political position under that king is believed to have led to its gradual adoption of Kshatriya status. He was a devotee of the Jain teachers Nemichandra and Ajitasena Bhattaraka, and a eulogy of him is inscribed on the Tyagada Brahmadeva Pillar at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shravanabelagola">Shravanabelagola</a>, whose base shows him with his guru Nemichandra.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ganga-dynasty">Western Gangas</a> were feudatories of the Rashtrakutas, and as their commander Chavundaraya fought many battles for those overlords, beginning in the days of the Rashtrakuta king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khottiga">Khottiga Amoghavarsha</a>. In the last years of Rashtrakuta rule the Gangas were threatened by civil war and by invasions of the Chola dynasty. When civil war broke out in 975 he supported Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rachamalla-iv">Rachamalla IV</a> and installed him on the throne.</p><p>In the same year he suppressed a rebellion by Panchaldeva Mahasamanta, and in the battle of Bageyur he killed Mudurachayya, avenging the death of his own brother Nagavarma. His exploits earned him titles such as Samara Parashurama and Vira Martanda. He remained influential into the reign of Rachamalla V, who ruled from 986 to 999.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The Chavundaraya Purana, also known as the Trishashtishalaka Mahapurana, is the second oldest existing prose work in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a>. It is based on two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> works, the Adipurana of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinasena">Jinasena</a> and the Uttarapurana of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunabhadra">Gunabhadra</a>, and tells the legends of sixty-three figures of Jain tradition: twenty-four tirthankaras, twelve chakravartis, nine balabhadras, nine narayanas and nine pratinarayanas. It was meant mainly for ordinary readers and avoided the complicated elements of Jain doctrine. He also wrote the Charitrasara in Sanskrit, and he patronised the Kannada grammarians Gunavarma and Nagavarma I and the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranna">Ranna</a>.</p><p>The statue of Bahubali that he commissioned stands on the Indragiri hill, also called Vindhyagiri. Carved from fine-grained white granite, it is 60 feet tall, with a face measuring 6.5 feet, and has no support up to the thighs. The Chavundaraya basadi on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragiri">Chandragiri</a> hill is credited to him by some scholars and to his son Jinadevana by others; another view holds that it was consecrated in the 11th century in his memory.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chavundaraya” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Resistance continued in the south, where the Western Ganga king Marasimha II and his general <b>Chavundaraya</b> backed Indra IV, a grandson of Krishna III, as Rashtrakuta claimant.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashtrakuta-dynasty">Rashtrakuta dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the south, Jainism flourished in Karnataka and Tamil Nadu in the first millennium CE under dynasties such as the Western Gangas, whose minister <b>Chavundaraya</b> had the great statue of Bahubali carved at Shravanabelagola around 983; Jain monks contributed greatly to early literature in Tamil and Kannada.</blockquote><a href="/religion/jainism">Jainism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 983 <b>Chavundaraya</b>, minister of Rachamalla IV, commissioned the 17-metre statue of Bahubali at Shravanabelagola, and Kannada literature flourished.</blockquote><a href="/state/western-ganga-dynasty">Western Ganga dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
